# Chapter 4: logs and markers
#
# A marker says "I care about this line of code". Deliberator then finds, in any fetched log, the lines that line of code
# wrote (logback's `logger:line`, or a stack frame), and the IDE highlights them and draws the marker in the gutter.
# This chapter marks the line of service A that logs "stored message", sends an order, fetches A's log and matches it.
# @env local
# @var corr = {{correlation}}
# @var msg = ord-{{uuid}}

### shell source-file
# Markers name files by absolute path; this checkout's path is whatever it is on your machine, so ask the shell.
# @cwd ..
# @capture inbox = out
realpath estate/services/service-a/src/main/java/estate/a/Inbox.java | tr -d '\n'

### op marker.add mark-inbox
# Line 58 of Inbox.java is `log.info("stored message id={} ...")`. Adding the same marker twice changes nothing.
# In the IDE: open Inbox.java, right-click the gutter of a line → Add Deliberator Marker does the same.
# @assert $[0].line > 0
{"file": "{{inbox}}", "line": 58, "logger": "estate.a.Inbox"}

### sqs send queue/inbox send-order
# @attr id={{msg}}
# @attr correlation={{corr}}
# @body ../estate/payloads/01-simple-order.json

### dynamodb get table/messages stored
# A stored it, so line 58 has just written a log line.
# @until $.status.S == RECEIVED within 20s
{"Key": {"id": {"S": "{{msg}}"}}}

### op logs.fetch a-log
# The whole of A's recent log, persisted as a read-only file of the session and hard-linked from the raw store.
# In the IDE: tool window → Services → select a → Log does this and opens the file with the marked lines highlighted.
# @assert $.lines > 0
# @capture log = $.path
{"target": "log/service-a", "since": "10m"}

### op logs.match matched
# Which lines of that file were written by a marked line of code: `n` is the line number in the file.
# In the IDE: in the log, select this run's id and right-click → Filter Log by Correlation to keep only this order's lines.
# @assert $.lines[0].n > 0
{"path": "{{log}}"}

### logs log/service-a stored-line
# The same line, live: a `logs` step with a regex filter. (It was armed before step 1, so it saw the line arrive.)
# @filter /stored message id={{msg}}/
# @for 3s
# @assert lines == 1

# To follow a log live in a terminal: `dlb logs fetch log/service-a --follow --since 1m --ws <ws>`, Ctrl-C to stop; it writes one
# file for the whole follow. In the IDE, the "Deliberator Service: a" run configuration shows A's log live in its console.
